        "content": "# EKS Architecture — Kubernetes Architecture and Core Concepts\n\n## Concept and technical explanation\n\nThis section examines Kubernetes Architecture and Core Concepts as it applies specifically to EKS Architecture. Understand\nthe underlying technology, the components involved, the inputs and outputs, and the\nconditions under which this feature is used in a real DevOps delivery environment.\n\n## Architecture and execution\n\nTrace the relevant component interactions. Identify the initiating component,\nprocessing component, configuration inputs, permissions, dependencies, resulting\nstate and observable outputs. Explain how the feature connects to the surrounding\npipeline stages.\n\n## Implementation\n\nImplement this concept using the actual technology and configuration relevant to\nEKS Architecture. Use the appropriate source files, commands, YAML, JSON, scripts or platform\nconfiguration. Explain each important setting and why it is required.\n\n## Practical verification\n\nExecute the implementation in a controlled environment. Verify the expected result\nusing command output, logs, API responses, generated artifacts, platform status or\nruntime behaviour. Distinguish a successful command from a genuinely healthy result.\n\n## Failure analysis and troubleshooting\n\nInvestigate realistic failures associated with EKS Architecture and Kubernetes Architecture and Core Concepts. Collect\nevidence, identify the responsible component, explain the root cause, apply a targeted\ncorrection and repeat verification. Avoid blind retries and destructive recovery.\n\n## Production application\n\nExplain how this capability is used in a production DevOps workflow. Cover security,\nreliability, maintainability, performance, operational ownership and its effect on\ndownstream pipeline stages."
